+We study an autonomous approximation of the complete parameter trajectory of
+fixed-finite-width deep nonlinear gradient flow.  The exact learned increment of
+each internal matrix is a time integral of a backward response against a forward
+response.  We project both endogenous response histories onto the first $P$
+shifted-Legendre modes while retaining the initialized matrices exactly.  Online
+Legendre projection and its triangular coefficient dynamics are inherited from
+polynomial-memory methods such as HiPPO; the neural construction couples the two
+projected histories through the reconstructed network.
+
+For arbitrary fixed depth, width, data and initialization, an
+ordinary residual-activity clock gives uniform physical-trajectory error
+$O_T(P^{-1})$ on every prescribed finite horizon.  A joint response-speed clock
+gives $O_T(P^{-2})$ under one additional activation derivative.  Neither result
+assumes successful fitting, Gaussian initialization or special input geometry.
+The second closure evolves $2(H-1)mnP$ history scalars and a shared symmetric
+$P\times P$ Gram matrix; its complete moving-state scaling under the sufficient
+order rule is
+$O(Hmn\varepsilon^{-1/2}+\varepsilon^{-1})$, before common outer blocks.
+
+This is a representation theorem for learned increments, not a net model-memory
+or runtime compression theorem.  Every initialized internal matrix, its forward
+and transpose actions, and fixed prefix data remain; reported implementations
+have larger minimal total state and are slower than dense flow in the large
+panels.  Existing circle and MNIST experiments show selected low-order
+matched-loss predictor fidelity, but they do not measure the same-time trajectory
+rates.  We separate the fixed-width theorem from width-uniform population
+closure, all-time tracking and compression of the initialized operator, none of
+which is proved.
 \end{abstract}
 
