+\paragraph{Feature-learning population and field descriptions.}
+Tensor Programs IV proves leading-order feature-learning limits under
+maximal-update parameterization at infinite width
+\citep{yang2021tensor}.  Its state consists of coordinate distributions and
+random variables; the explicit finite-step recursion includes variables indexed
+by earlier training steps.  Its limit is taken at each fixed discrete training
+time, and the paper supplies an algorithm and experiments, rather than a
+fixed-width parameter-trajectory approximation.
+
+Feature-learning dynamical mean-field theory represents infinite-width training
+by self-consistent two-time activation, gradient and response kernels
+\citep{bordelon2022dmft}.  On a grid with $M$ samples and $N_t$ time points,
+its Table~1 reports $O(M^2N_t^2)$ kernel memory and $O(M^3N_t^3)$ kernel time;
+it provides an alternating sampling algorithm and finite-network experiments,
+while its derivation is not a fixed-finite-width trajectory theorem.  Its
+finite-width extension gives perturbative $N^{-1/2}$ fluctuations and
+$N^{-1}$ mean corrections around those order parameters
+\citep{bordelon2023finite}, not an exact closure of one finite realization.
+
+Rigorous multilayer mean field evolves functions
+$w_1(t,c_1)$, $w_\ell(t,c_{\ell-1},c_\ell)$ and $b_\ell(t,c_\ell)$ on a fixed
+neuronal ensemble and couples wide finite networks to this autonomous flow on
+compact horizons \citep{nguyen2023rigorous}.  Neural Feature Flow similarly
+evolves feature measures and pair functions on their supports and gives a
+finite-width approximation under its initialization, width and step-size
+conditions \citep{fang2020features}.  These systems have finitely many field
+types but infinitely many scalar degrees of freedom; they are width-limit
+descriptions, not fixed-width matrix compression.  Shallow Wasserstein and
+distributional limits provide related one- or two-layer precedents
+\citep{chizat2018global,sirignano2019lln,mei2018meanfield}.
+
+\paragraph{Lazy limits, initialization theories and projection closure.}
+NNGP, signal-propagation and dynamical-isometry results concern random
+initialization and its input--output statistics
+\citep{lee2018nngp,schoenholz2017deep,pennington2017resurrecting}.
+The NTK and wide-network linearization give infinite-width or sufficiently wide
+lazy-regime function dynamics in which hidden-feature motion vanishes
+\citep{jacot2018ntk,lee2019wide}.  They locate a different regime rather than
+attempt the present closure.  More broadly, Mori--Zwanzig projection writes
+resolved dynamics as Markovian, memory and unresolved terms
+\citep{chorin2000optimal}; it is conceptual memory-closure precedent, not a
+neural trajectory theorem.
